From fharrell at virginia.edu  Sun May  2 11:53:12 1999
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Sun, 02 May 1999 07:53:12 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Regression with order constraints on regression coefficients
In-Reply-To: <B9E51B9719BDD111AB640000F80197CDBEB171@rplmsem2.pal.roche.com>
Message-ID: <000c01be9492$5c8049c0$7ed5bfa8@fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu>

In addition to the formal approach recommended by Brian Ripley,
Tibshirani's "bootstrap bumping" is worth a try.  Here one generates
say 100 model fits using bootstrap resampling and takes the subset
of those fits for which the estimates were in the desired order.
[Note: if this subset is small, that is evidence that the order
constraint is not consistent with the data.]  Of the subset, pick
the fit that has the best log likelihood when evaluated on the
ORIGINAL sample.  I haven't seen anyone try this approach on a
problem exactly like yours Chris, but I think Tibshirani has used
it for a smooth regression fit with monotonicity constraint.

See

@TECHREPORT{tib97mod,
  author = {Tibshirani, Robert and Knight, Keith},
  year = 1997,
  title = {Model search and inference by bootstrap ``bumping''},
  note = {Presented at the Joint Statistical Meetings, Chicago, August 1996},
  address = {{\tt http://utstat.toronto.edu/tibs}},
  institution = {Department of Statistics, University of Toronto},
  annote = {simultaneous confidence regions; confidence sets; constrained
           optimization; CART; improving performance of estimators via the
           bootstrap}
}

The Design bootcov function can help with bootstrap bumping in conjunction
with the Design "oosl" method ('out of sample' log likelihood).

-Frank Harrell

