From rdiazuri at students.wisc.edu  Sun May  2 12:56:20 1999
From: rdiazuri at students.wisc.edu (ramon diaz-uriarte)
Date: Sun, 02 May 1999 07:56:20 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] SPLUS procedures for Paired Survival Data
In-Reply-To: <B9E51B9719BDD111AB640000F80197CDBEB170@rplmsem2.pal.roche.com>
References: <B9E51B9719BDD111AB640000F80197CDBEB170@rplmsem2.pal.roche.com>
Message-ID: <3.0.2.32.19990502075620.00a35d20@students.wisc.edu>

The following references might be useful:

Lee, E. W., Wei, L. J., and Amato, D. A. 1992. Cox-type regression analysis
for large numbers of small groups of correlated failure time observations.
Pages 237-242 in  Klein, J. P. and Goel, P. K., eds., Survival analysis:
state of the art. Kluwer Academic Publishers, The Netherlands.
Lin, D. Y. MULCOX2: a general computer program for the Coxregression
analysis of multivariate failure time data. Comp. Meth. Prog. Biomed. 1993,
40: 279-293.
Wei, L. J., Lin, D. Y., and Weissfeld, L. 1989. Regression analysis of
multivariate incomplete failure time data by modeling marginal
distributions. Journal of the American Statistical Association, 84: 1065-1073

What they suggest is very easy to fit with SPlus; the following commands
will test the multivariate hypothesis (most of these come straight from the
docummentation for survival4 --e.g., p. 20-36 of the SPlus v. 3.3. for Win
manual; I just added the last three lines) .  You will want to change the
model, etc.

mult.cox<-coxph(Surv(Time,Status)~ (....+Trt+....) * strata(TypeVar) +
cluster (ID),data=exp1.new,method='exact')
# i.e., the type of response variable is entered as a stratum
mult.cox #where are coeffs. for treatment?
Trt<-c(3,6) #where coefficients for treatment are in mult.cox
cmat<-diag(2);cmat[,1]<-1 #obtain contrast matrix
nu<-cmat%*%mult.cox$coef[Trt] #the vector nu, as in p. 1066 of Wei et al.
(WLW).
wvar<-cmat%*%mult.cox$var[Trt,Trt]%*%t(cmat)  #variance matrix
sqrt(diag(wvar)) #standard dev. (as in Table 5 of WLW).
W<-t(nu)%*%solve(wvar)%*%nu 
#obtaining the quadr. form W (p. 1066); this is the one used to test the
multiv. hypoth that there is no treatment effect
1-pchisq(W,length(nu)) # chi-square test of multiv. hypoth.
StEstim<-nu/sqrt(diag(wvar)) #the standardized estimates of the
effects.(e.g., WLW, p. 1070).

Ramon

