Neither.  I have one large (16.8 GB) hard-drive, partitioned into logical
drives c: - h:.  Splus (and all statistics programs) are installed on the
d: drive.  All utilities are installed on the g: drive.  Since I use
editpad for various functions, I left it in a directory called "util",
which sits under the root of g:.  There is a path statement in the
autoexec.bat file directing traffic to "g:\util" for utilities.  (This same
configuration occurs on all 3 of my systems, though the others have smaller
hard drives).

I used Notepad as the editor and pager until I got bored with its limits.
I use Editpad for most everything else.  Consequently, I decided to
configure SPlus to use Editpad as its editor too.  This is when the fun
began.  With the editpad executable in g:\util, and Splus appropriately
notified of its location, it takes nearly a minute of watching the
hourglass cursor (on, respectively, a 200 MHz Pentium Pro, a 366 MHz PII,
and a 300 MHz PII) before editpad appears with the function to "fix".  It
took almost no time at all for notepad to find the functions to "fix".
This occurs on all three systems (all Dells - two running Win 95, one
running Win 98).  If I move Editpad to the c:\Windows directory, Splus
finds it pretty quickly, but nowhere near as quickly as it does if I rename
editpad.exe as notepad.exe.

Since I have no previous experience with this problem, *AND* because I find
it on three different systems (at least two of which hadn't been touched
for weeks, until two days ago when I encountered this problem), it is hard
to argue that something in Splus has become corrupt.  However, to satisfy
myself that this wasn't the case, I tried various "fixes" to test the
hypothesis:  1)  I deleted the _sum4.txt, _sum4i.txt, and _sum4.tx_ files.
(No change); 2) I backed up the _Prefs directory and let Splus build a new
one, to which I made a single change (viz. to change the editor to Editpad
[on the g: drive]) (no help); and 3) finally I created a new working
directory for Splus data, thereby avoiding anything corrupt that might
still be lingering in the old _Data\_nonfi.

None of these things have sped up accessing Editpad.

Now, assuming Editpad itself was the culprit, I decided to try the emacs
that came with Splus.  This resides in the *same* directory as the Splus
executable.  Emacs takes nearly as long to load as Editpad, and both take
at least 20 times longer to load than notepad.  Again, this time is reduced
significantly if emacs is moved to the \windows directory.

I realize that others don't have this problem, which is why Mathsoft tech
support is being copied on this email.  I'm completely out of ideas for
getting to the root of this problem.

