>but if you do decide to buy it
>I would be interested in your opinion.

I am still working on this problem. I might post further questions.

In the mean time I have used a simple ad-hoc method which would not be
considered a GARCH model (I don't think?).

I preprocess the returns by dividing by the 20 day exponential
volatility:

var(i) = lamda * r(i)^2 + (1-lamd) * var(i-1)    ; lamda=1/20
vol(i) = sqrt(var(i))
z(i) = r(i) / vol(i)

The preprocessed returns are very close to being normally distributed.
This is because as r(i) -> infinity, z(i) -> sqrt(20)
This elimintes problems with outliers.

I then model the "covariance" matrix as a 1 year exponential moving
average:

cov(i,,j,t) = gamma * z(i,t)*z(j,t) + (1-gamma) * cov(i,j,t-1)

The diagonal of the matrix is close to 1, but not exactly.

I use the Lanczos / Arnoldi method to take the first few (~20)
eigenvectors of the matrix. This was extremely difficult to do. I used
the ARPACK library from RICE university to do this calculation. During
typical markets the first component explains about 10% of the variance.
During market crashes such as 1987, the first component explains about
%40 of the variance.

It was very difficult to get Arpack to compile and run with Splus4.5
(for WIN-32 / 95/98/NT). I plan to post some of my makefiles and patches
on the Powersoft bulleten board. Arpack has a dubuging / tracing utility
wich uses extension screen IO. However Splus4.5 does not allow Watcom
Fortran to print to the screen. I wrote my own Fortran utility, (and
rewrote some of the Watcom libraries) to do this.

I am also looking at the E3SU model from BARRA.

Thanks to all for your support,

Clark Sims

