Message-ID: <04256769.005B3277.00@Hermes.Khronos.Com>

>The data set that I wanted to input is this
>
>
>1 johnny,1971      11                 21      weeks        4.8    4.0
>1 Anhalt,1971      12                 10      weeks        15.9  4.2
>2 Benjams,1993  493(189/154)  97.25  6-7x/week   8.2   1.4
>
>There are 7 columns.
>The code that I used to read this data set,
>read.table(file="growth.txt",sep=c(1,3,16,27,34,44,49))
>
>The output that I got is the following, where the columns
>are not readin properly.
>
>             V1          V3      V4        V5   V6  V7
> johnny,1971  1          11      21     weeks  4.8 4.0
> Anhalt,1971  1          12      10     weeks 15.9 4.2
>Benjams,1993  2 493(189/154 ) 97.25 6-7x/week  8.2 1.4

Your problem appears to be that you are not telling S-Plus to ignore row names.
In that case it tries to guess at what is the appropriate column names to use.
If you look at the row.names parameter desription in the read.table help file
entry it says:

row.names
------------
optional specification of the row names for the data frame. If provided, it can
give the actual row names, as a vector of length equal to the number of rows, or
it can be a single number or character string. In the latter case, the argument
indicates which variable in the data frame to use as row names (the variable
will then be dropped from the frame). If row.names is missing, the function will
use the first nonnumeric field with no duplicates as the row names. If no such
field exists, the row names are 1:nrow(x). You can force this last version,
regardless of suitable fields to use as row names, by giving row.names=NULL. Row
names, wherever they come from, must be unique.
------------

Notice that if you don't tell it to not process row names (by explictily saying
row.names=NULL) it will use the "first nonnumeric field with no duplicates as
the row names", in this case your second column.

So specificy row.names=NULL, specify a vector of row names, or specificy
explicitly which column you want to use for row names.

Regards,

Rafael Mayer
