From Karen_Johnson at bdhq.bd.com  Thu May  6 17:52:35 1999
From: Karen_Johnson at bdhq.bd.com
Date: Thu, 06 May 1999 13:52:35 -0400
Subject: [S] double integration - summary
Message-ID: <85256769.005BA3F1.00@bdhq.bd.com>

I had posted a question on double integrating a sloppy function.  I
received a range of answers, all of which are interesting and are appended
the end.  None did all that I wanted, but I was able to develop a function
that did building on solutions from Bill Venables and Nick Ellis.

What I really wanted was a function styled after and built on the existing
integrate function (to take advantage of the Fortran and pre-existing error
checks).  Therefor a double integrate function would have the following
arguments:
   the integrand function,
   the lower and upper limits of the inner integral,
   the lower and upper limits of the outer integral, and
   ... for passing additional arguments to the integrand function (this
   especially desirable to generate graphs of F(x, y) vs a third argument -
   of course it would be even better if a vector of constants could be
   inputted generating a vector of results, but integrate() can't do that
   either).

I have written a function that accomplishes all of the above, but it was a
struggle and the result is among the nastiest coding I've ever seen.  Any
suggestions gratefully excepted!

integrate.double <- function(f, lower.outer, upper.outer, sub.outer = 100,
   lower.inner, upper.inner, sub.inner = 100, ...){

   fun1 <- function(x2, x.outer.name, opt.arg.char, arg.list){
     unlist(lapply(x2,
        function(x2, x.outer.name, opt.arg.char, arg.list)
          eval(parse(text = paste("integrate(
             f = arg.list$integrand.fun,
             lower = eval(arg.list$lower),
             upper = eval(arg.list$upper),
             subdivisions = arg.list$subdivisions,",
             x.outer.name, "= x2,",
             opt.arg.char,
             ")$integral"))),
        x.outer.name = x.outer.name,
        opt.arg.char = opt.arg.char,
        arg.list = arg.list
     ))
   }

   x.outer.name <- unpaste(deparse(args(f), short = T)[2], sep = " =
   ")[[1]]
   opt.arg.char <- list(...)
   if(length(opt.arg.char) == 0) opt.arg.char <- NULL
   else opt.arg.char <- paste(names(opt.arg.char), unlist(opt.arg.char),
     sep = " = ", collapse = ", ")
   arg.list <- list(
     integrand.fun = f,
     lower = substitute(lower.inner),
     upper = substitute(upper.inner),
     subdivisions = sub.inner)

   integrate(f = fun1,
     lower = lower.outer, upper = upper.outer, subdivisions = sub.outer,
     x.outer.name = x.outer.name, opt.arg.char = opt.arg.char,
     arg.list = arg.list)$integral
}

# some simple tests
> a.fun <- function(x1, x2) x1 + x2
> integrate.double(f = a.fun,
     lower.outer = 0.5, upper.outer = 1, lower.inner = 0, upper.inner = 5)
[1] 8.125

> a3.fun <- function(x1, x2, alpha) x1 + x2 + alpha
> integrate.double(f = a3.fun,
     lower.outer = 0.5, upper.outer = 1, lower.inner = 0, upper.inner = 5,
alpha = 3)
[1] 15.625
> integrate.double(f = a3.fun,
     lower.outer = 0.5, upper.outer = 1, lower.inner = 1 - x2, upper.inner
= x2,
     alpha = 2)
[1] 0.8333333

> a7.fun <- function(x.in, x.out, alpha1, alpha2)
     x.in + x.out + alpha1 + alpha2
> integrate.double(f = a7.fun,
     lower.outer = 0.5, upper.outer = 1, lower.inner = 0, upper.inner = 5,
     alpha1 = 1, alpha2 = 2)
[1] 15.625

The opt.arg.char part bothers me the most.  It was needed because I
couldn't pass the ... all the way to the integrand.fun otherwise.  (I
learned alot about what ... isn't, I'm not convinced I know what it is
.....)

Thanks to everyone that took the time to answer, especially T. Diaz for
fowarding replies to his similiar quesiton.
KJJ
karen_johnson at bd.com

>>From Bill Venables:
<snip>
The important thing to remember is that the inner integral has to
be a function of two variables, *vectorized* in the sense that
given a vector of values for the outer variable of integration it
has to supply a vector of results for the inner integral, (if you
get my drift...)  This is what the unlist(lapply(...)) is all about.

double.integral.demo.fn <- function() {
  fun <- function(y)
    unlist(lapply(y,
                  function(y) {
                    integrate(f = function(x, y) x+y,
                              lower = 1-y,
                              upper = y,
                     y = y)$integral
                  }))
  integrate(f = fun,
            lower = 0.5,
            upper = 1)$integral
}
<snip>

>>From Nick Ellis:
+ res<-0*y
+ for(i in 1:length(res)){
+ res[i] <-
integrate(function(x,z){x+z},lower=1-y[i],upper=y[i],z=y[i])$integral
+ }
+ res
+ }
> integrate(Inner.integral,.5,1)$integral
[1] 0.3333333

The function you are integrating has to have vector value, hence the loop.

>>From Douglas Wiens:
Here's a function I wrote to do Simpson's rule integration over the
rectangle
[lx,ux] x [ly,uy].  One has to create a matrix 'mat' whose (i,j)th element
is
f(x[i],y[j])
, outside of the function..  Of course one could modify it so
thatt
one used the name of the function as one of the arguments and then had
'mat'
created within the integrationfunction.  I haven't done that.

It's worked well for me.  I typically use a 'mat' with 101 rows and
columns, if
integrating over something like [-1,1] x [-1,1].

simp2 <- function(mat,lx=-1,ux=1,ly=-1,uy=1) {
     # mat is a matrix whose (i,j)th element is f(x[i],y[j])
     # Number of rows and number of columns are both odd
     # Example: x <- seq(from=1,to=2,length=101);
        #          y<-seq(from=1,to=2,length=101)
     #          g <- function(x,y) log(x+2*y); mat <- outer(x,y,g)
     
           simp2(mat,1,2,1,2)

Mx <- nrow(mat)-1
hx <- (ux-lx)/Mx
dx <- c(1,rep(c(4,2),Mx/2-1),4,1)

My <- ncol(mat)-1
hy <- (uy-ly)/My
dy <- c(1,rep(c(4,2),My/2-1),4,1)

hx*hy*sum(outer(dx,dy)*mat)/9
}

>>From Steve Wofsy:
suggest you consider Monte Carlo; it usually works very well for this
type of problem

