From wegelin at stat.washington.edu  Fri May  7 23:34:56 1999
From: wegelin at stat.washington.edu (Jacob A. Wegelin)
Date: Fri, 07 May 1999 16:34:56 -0700
Subject: [S] union and intersection of sets
Message-ID: <Pine.SGI.3.96.990507155915.19482B-100000@sigma1.stat.washington.edu>

The following two functions are more general than the ones shipped with
S+.  Please let me know if you see a problem with them, or if there are
better functions floating around that do the same thing.

Thanks

Jake

"union"<-
function(...)
{
        unique(c(...))
}

"intersect"<-
function(...)
{
# The standard intersect function works for only two sets at a time.  
# Also Bill Dunlap's email of Tue, 6 Dec 94 17:17:21 PST to s-news says the 
"sets" have to be
# real "sets" in the sense of not having repeated values.  
# This function should work on objects with repeated values, an arbitrary 
number of "sets".  
        stuff <- list(...)
        answer <- unique(stuff[[1]])
        for(i in 2:length(stuff)) {
                answer <- answer[match(unique(stuff[[i]]), answer, nomatch = 0)]
        }
        answer
}

references:

standard union() and intersect() functions in S+ 3.4

