From simon at tamarama.stanford.edu  Sun May  9 22:56:07 1999
From: simon at tamarama.stanford.edu (Simon Jackman)
Date: Sun, 09 May 1999 15:56:07 -0700
Subject: [S] system of regression equations, faster?
Message-ID: <99050916240800.03817@tamarama>

Dear List,

Consider working with a system of regression equations, something along
the lines of

y_1 = X_1 beta_1 + e_1
y_2 = X_2 beta_2 + e_2

with (e_1, e_2)' joint Normal with var-covar matrix Sigma %*% I_n
(the Kronecker product signified by the ".*." operator).  Most
textbooks stack the y vectors, make a big block diagonal X matrix, and
give the least squares estimate of \beta = (\beta_1, \beta_2) as

(X'GX)^{-1} X'Gy

where G is Sigma^{-1} .*. I_n

Implementing this as written is painful in Splus, since forming X'G takes
ages.  For a problem I am working on now, my (block diagonal) X matrix is
909 by 38, in a two-equation system (I'd like the code to be reasonably
general, for when I have more than 2 eqns).  This is in the context of a
simulation, in which Sigma changes over iterations; based on other
considerations I need about 10,000 iterations, each requiring the awful
X'G term, and then of course the inversion of X'GX.

I moved to gauss for this, which is faster, and runs into less memory
problems over simulations, but is still very slow.

Finally, my qn: surely there is a better way to program the computations
here?  This has to be one of those situations where direct implementation
of textbook formula is not the smartest way to proceed...

Any suggestions?  In some recent list traffic on forming block diagonal
matrices I recall Bill Venables (hi there) suggesting that there are
faster ways to do the things for which we typically think of using
block-diagonals...  

Thanks -- Simon Jackman
