> 
> I moved to gauss for this, which is faster, and runs into less memory
> problems over simulations, but is still very slow.
> 
> Finally, my qn: surely there is a better way to program the computations
> here?  This has to be one of those situations where direct implementation
> of textbook formula is not the smartest way to proceed...

Are there any such situations?

> Any suggestions?  In some recent list traffic on forming block diagonal
> matrices I recall Bill Venables (hi there) suggesting that there are
> faster ways to do the things for which we typically think of using
> block-diagonals...  

Well, let's start with:

Linear equations are not solved numerically by matrix inversion.
Regressions are not solved by the textbook formula betahat = (X'X)^{-1}X'y

so don't confuse algebraic and numerical solutions.  S-PLUS uses the QR
decomposition.

In your problem you can write e_1 = a* eta and e_2 as b * eta + c * zeta,
where eta and zeta are independent N(0,1). (Just write down the correlation
matrices and equate them. More generally, use the standard ways to simulate
multivariate normals via Choleski.)  Then you have

y_1 = X_1 beta_1 + a*eta
y_2 = X_2 beta_2 + b*eta +c*zeta

and now maximize the log likelihood, equivalently minimize the sum of 
squares of eta plus the sum of squares of zeta.  You will find a
set of equations which only involve  X_i'X_j, i= 1,2.

Alternatively, and closer to your orginal problem, try writing out
X'GX and X'GY as

X'GX = sum_{ij} g_{ij} X_i'X_j
X'GY = sum_{ij} g_{ij} X_i'Y_j

which should be easy to compute (and remember the symmetries).

Once you have your equations, look into other ways to solve linear
equations: unless doing them by hand, matrix inversion is never a good
idea.

