From david.h.pattison at ssa.gov  Mon May 10 13:41:34 1999
From: david.h.pattison at ssa.gov
Date: Mon, 10 May 1999 08:41:34 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary: Filling in a matrix with values
Message-ID: <9905109263.AA926340416@ssagw07.ssa.gov>

     Thanks to all who responded.  The question, briefly, was, given a 
     matrix v, and given three vectors i, j, and vij with, respectively, a 
     set of row indices, a set of column indices, and a set of values to go 
     with the corresponding row and column indivies, how do I insert these 
     values into the appropriate elements of v?  I had found that
     
        v[i,j] <- vij
     
     does not work.
     
     The answer, supplied, by David Caccia, Phil Spector, Nick Ellis, Brian 
     Ripley, and Bill Dunlap, is to index with a matrix built with cbind:
     
        v[cbind(i,j)] <- vij .
     
     This does the job.
     
     Bill Dunlap also explained what is going on.  (And Patrick Burns 
     supplied a pointer to his Spoetry, which also does some explaining.)
     
     For my particular problem I am only inserting values into the upper 
     triangle of the matrix v and then copying these values into the lower 
     triangle.  Phil Spector and Brian Ripley offered code for doing this.  
     From Spector, from a matrix v that had started as zeroes and has had the 
     upper triangle inserted as above:
     
        d <- diag(v)
        v <- v + t(v)
        diag(v) <- v .
     
     From Ripley, starting with anything at all in the lower triangle:
     
        v[lower.tri(v)] <- t(v)[lower.tri(v)] .
     
     What I am doing, by the way, is plotting some 54 by 54 covariance and 
     correlation matrices.  The problem is such that it make sense to look 
     at plots of these matrices, and
     
        persp(v),
     
     where v is a covariance or a correlation matrix, makes beautiful plots 
     very simply, giving a sort of covariance surface.  My data is such 
     that I do not have covariances for the far corners of the covariance 
     matrix.  The persp function has the handy characteristic that persp(v) 
     will simply leave out from the surface any elements of v that are NA.  
     Hence I start with a matrix of NAs, insert into the upper triangle the 
     covariances I have calculated from the data, flip the upper triangle 
     into the lower triangle, and plot with persp.
     
     David Pattison                            David.H.Pattison at ssa.gov 
     Social Security Administration
     
     
     
     

