From feldesmanm at pdx.edu  Mon May 10 23:20:37 1999
From: feldesmanm at pdx.edu (Marc R. Feldesman)
Date: Mon, 10 May 1999 16:20:37 -0700
Subject: [S] Function advice
Message-ID: <4.1.19990510161145.00994b20@pop4.ibm.net>

Slowly I'm beginning to grasp the vast territory of SPlus and am writing
small functions to do things I need done.  The function coded is intended
to calculate the Mahalanobis D^2 distances between cases and their
respective group centroid.  The function is coded quite primitively now -
it works.  I use this type of function a lot in my work so I'd like to make
it more efficient, if possible.  With a 2500 case file (30 variables and 30
groups) the function is reasonably fast (< 10 seconds) on a 300 MHz PII
machine.

I'd appreciate it if an SPlus guru or two might offer suggestions on ways
to make the code more efficient.  I am sure, for example, there is a better
way to code what is now in a "for" loop, though I had some trouble figuring
out which of the various "apply"-family functions would accomplish this.
Similarly, I've "split" the file 3 times, which seems inefficient to me.

Suggestions welcomed and thank you for your time.

mahal<-function(x, y)
{
        # x is data matrix 
        # y is grouping variable
        
        xy.vcv <- lapply(split(x, y), var)  #get group vcv matrices
        xy.centroids <- lapply(split(x,y), colMeans)  #get variable means by 
groups

        # make list out of data

        xy.list<-list(c(split(x,y)))

        for (i in 1:length(xy.list)
        {
                xy.mahal<-lapply(xy.list[[i]], function(x,y,z)
                 mahalanobis(x, y, z), xy.centroids[[i]], xy.vcv[[i]])
        }
        return(xy.mahal)
}

