From therneau at mayo.edu  Tue May 11 17:54:46 1999
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.)
Date: Tue, 11 May 1999 12:54:46 -0500
Subject: [S] Rpart bug
Message-ID: <199905111754.MAA11693@rocky.Mayo.EDU>

  A bug has been found in the "rpart" routines.  The fix is to change line
61 in graycode.c per below

        else {
            temp = val[i];
-->         for (j=i-1; j>=0 && val[j]>temp; j--) {
                gray[j+1] = gray[j];
                val[j+1] = val[j];
                }
            val[j+1] = temp;
            gray[j+1] =i;

to
            for (j=i-1; j>=k && val[j]>temp; j--) {

This error only occurs for
        a categorical predictor x
        y has exactly 2 categories, and method='class' is used
        we are at a lower level of the tree, and not all categories of x
                are represented
        unlucky values in a scratch array       
 
Example:
   Two continuous variables x2 and x3, two factor variables f1 and f3.
   Notice that in node 6 (the last one listed), it shows variable f3
splits as "--LLR-", however, only categories 4 and 5 of predictor f3
exist in that node, based on the first split.  The output is correct
in node 3, however.

> fit4 <- rpart(y ~ x2 + x3 + f1 + f3, fdata, method='class')
> summary(fit4)

Node number 1: 300 observations,    complexity param=0.1933
  predicted class= 1  expected loss= 0.5 
     class counts:  150 150 
    probabilities:  0.5 0.5 
  left son=2 (39 obs) right son=3 (261 obs)
  Primary splits:
      f3 splits as  LLLRRL, improve=12.390, (0 missing)
      f1 splits as  RRRLR, improve= 3.256, (0 missing)
      x3 < 37.5 to the left,  improve= 2.179, (0 missing)
      x2 < 33.5 to the left,  improve= 1.502, (0 missing)

Node number 2: 39 observations
  predicted class= 1  expected loss= 0.1282 
     class counts:  34  5 
    probabilities:  0.8718 0.1282 
 
Node number 3: 261 observations,    complexity param=0.06667
  predicted class= 2  expected loss= 0.4444 
     class counts:  116 145 
    probabilities:  0.4444 0.5556 
  left son=6 (66 obs) right son=7 (195 obs)
  Primary splits:
      f1 splits as  LRRLL, improve=3.0460, (0 missing)
      x3 < 21.5 to the left,  improve=1.9970, (0 missing)
      x2 < 33.5 to the left,  improve=1.5300, (0 missing)
      f3 splits as  ---RL-, improve=0.1642, (0 missing)
 
Node number 6: 66 observations,    complexity param=0.02
  predicted class= 1  expected loss= 0.4242 
     class counts:  38 28 
    probabilities:  0.5758 0.4242 
  left son=12 (10 obs) right son=13 (56 obs)
  Primary splits:
      x3 < 21.5 to the left,  improve=2.4780, (0 missing)
      x2 < 33.5 to the left,  improve=2.4370, (0 missing)
      f1 splits as  L-LLR, improve=0.8859, (0 missing)
      f3 splits as  --LLR-, improve=0.1980, (0 missing)
 

Details:
   In the code shown above, the contents of the vector "val" are undefined
for categories with no members (numbers 1,2, 3 and 6 for variable f3 in
node 6, for example).  Actually, it then contains whatever number was last
stored into this scratch array, when it was last used in another section
of the rpart code.  With the bug, and the appropriate "leftover" entry in
the val vector, an incorrect sorting of the categories results.
   1. An "L" will appear instead of a "-" for one or more categories.
   2. Not all of the legal splits will have been examined, so the
improvement may be an underestimate.

   Thanks to Ad Feelders, who pointed out the bug to me and provided a
data example.  

        Terry Therneau

   PS. For those of you who depend on the pre-compiled code (Windows), Brian
R intends to update it this week.
