From Todd.Taylor at pnl.gov  Wed May 12 15:47:04 1999
From: Todd.Taylor at pnl.gov (Z. Todd Taylor)
Date: Wed, 12 May 1999 08:47:04 -0700
Subject: Re: [S] Manuals
In-Reply-To: <199905121427.JAA16666@rocky.Mayo.EDU>
Message-ID: <199905121547.IAA08297@aggie.pnl.gov>

"Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D." <therneau at mayo.edu> (Terry Therneau) wrote:

>    My copies of the Splus reference manuals are getting dog-eared and
> worn, and I'd like to get new ones.  When I mention this to Statsci the
> usual reaction is surprise: "why not use the online ones?".  The answer is
> that I like printed material better.
>   Am I that unusual?  Is there sufficient market to make publishing the
> manuals worthwhile?

The notion that online help files can substitute for good
printed documentation is silly, IMHO.  The two mediums serve
different purposes, requiring different designs; neither should
be neglected.

I get especially frustrated with printed "manuals" that simply
mimic the online docs (i.e., tell you to click here, drag there,
etc., without ever explaining the systematic concepts).

Unfortunately, in response to your last question, I *don't*
think there is enough market demand to move developers to take
printed documentation seriously.  Today's users just fail (or,
IMO, refuse) to recognize the value of understanding how things
work, preferring rather to nose around clicking buttons until
they get a desirable effect.  (But I digress...)

--Todd
-- 
Z. Todd Taylor
Pacific Northwest National Laboratory
Todd.Taylor at pnl.gov
Why is it unable and inability, not inable and unability?
