Another vote from Mayo for printed manuals.  That is frequently one of
the complaints I get from new users (stops some of them from going much
further with the language).  Currently there is Venables & Ripley,
which is good for some purposes but doesn't cover everything, there is
Chambers & Hastie which again is good for some purposes, there is the
original blue book (Becker/Chambers/Wilks) which has some examples that
I still turn to occasionally, and there are the reference manuals.

One reason I like the reference manuals is that I'm used as a resource
person for Splus in our group.  Frequently someone comes to me with a
question and it is easier to point to something on paper than starting
up Splus, finding the helpfile, and explaining everything off of the
screen.

