From mbwigg at navigator.math.uwaterloo.ca  Wed May 12 18:54:01 1999
From: mbwigg at navigator.math.uwaterloo.ca (Melanie Wigg)
Date: Wed, 12 May 1999 14:54:01 -0400
Subject: [S] Summary: adjusting axes on plots
Message-ID: <199905121854.OAA12854@navigator.math.uwaterloo.ca>

Splus users,

Many thanks to all who responded to my question regarding
"cutting out" a section of an axis on a plot.  Your
suggestions were very helpful and solved my problem!

Below is my original post, followed by a summary of the
responses I received.

Melanie Wigg
mbwigg at navigator.math.uwaterloo.ca

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Question:
---------

I'm using Splus 3.4 on Unix and have a question regarding
adjusting the axes of my plot.  Specifically, I would
like to "cut out" a section of the possible values on
a certain axis.  For example, the x axis might be ...

      |
      |
------|------|------|----^^^----|------|------|------------
      0      10     20          80    90     100

Does anyone know of a way to do this?

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Yuelin Li suggested the following method for adjusting how the
axis is displayed, using a combination of axis() and mtext()
functions.  Note that this will leave a "hole" in the x axis,
but won't adjust how points are plotted on the graph.

plot(1:100, 1:100, type = "n", axes = F, xlab = "", ylab = "")
par(tck = 0)  # temporarily cancel tick marks
axis(1, at = c(17, 100), label = F, tick = T) # tick=T to plot axis 1
axis(1, at = c(0, 13), label = F, tick = T)
mtext("/", side = 1, line = -1.5, at = 17, cex = 2.5)
mtext("/", side = 1, line = -1.5, at = 13, cex = 2.5)
par(tck = -0.02)  # put the tick mark back
axis(1, at = 0, label = T, tick = T)
axis(1, at = seq(20, 100, 20), label = seq(40, 120, 20), tick = T)

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bill Dunlap suggested defining a function to convert the data to a linear
axis, say with values from 0 to 1, as follows:

> f <- function(x, xlim = c(0, 100), xbreak = c(25, 75))
{
        ifelse(x < xbreak[1], (x - xlim[1])/(diff(xlim) - diff(xbreak)), ifelse(
                x > xbreak[2], 1 - (xlim[2] - x)/(diff(xlim) - diff(xbreak)), 
                NA))
}

Then use f() whenever you want to put things along the x axis.  E.g.,

> ticks<-c(0,10,20,80,90,100)
> x<-runif(30,0,100)                     
> y<-3*x+runif(30,0,10)
> plot(f(x),y,axes=F)
> axis(2) # normal y axis
> axis(1, at=f(ticks), lab=ticks) # broken x axis
> box()
> abline(lty=2, v=f(c(25,75)))

This f() doesn't put a gap between 25 and 75 but could be modified to do
so.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rod Tjoelker suggested plotting the 2 parts separately as follows:

One way to do a plot with a "cut out" section is to
plot each part separately.  Plot the first part with
an extended x-axis, then add the second part.  You 
could add the second part by recalculting the plotting
points (subtract a constant from the x-values) or by
using the "subplot" function.

I've not tested the code below, but it should be close
enough to get you on your way with some adjustments.

part1 <- x < lower.cutpoint
part2 <- x > upper.cutpoint
shift <- upper.cutpoint - lower.cutpoint - (0.04 * diff(range(x)))

plot(x[part1], y[part1], axes=F, xlim=c(min(x),max(x)-shift),type="l")
axis(2)
axis(1, at=pretty(x[part1]))
points(x[part2] - shift, y[part2], type="l")
xp <- pretty(x[part2])
axis(1, at=xp-shift, labels=xp)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Brian Ripley suggested doing two plots side by side:

I think you need to do this by hand. What you effectively have is two plots
side-by-side with a common Y axis.  So you could actually do this as two
plots with the axes suppressed on the second (par(axes=F)), and put the
second (80 -- 100) axis in by a call to axis.  You will either need
a gap between the axes or draw something there.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Karen Johnson gave the following example: 

In this example the actual x-axis goes from 0 to 1.1 - the extra 0.1 is for
the gap, and the data is scaled accordingly.

   # make fake data
   set.seed(8)
   x <- round(c(runif(10, 1, 25), runif(10, 75, 100)), 0)
   y <- round(rnorm(20, 10, 4), 0)

   # set up plot
   plot(0, 0, type='n', xaxt='n', xaxs='s', xlim=c(0, 1.1), xlab='x',
     ylim=range(c(0, y)), ylab='y', bty='n')
   abline(v=0)

   lines(c(0, 0.5+0.03), c(0, 0))
   axis(1, at=pretty(c(0, 25))*2/100, labels=pretty(c(0, 25)), ticks=F)
   points(x[x < 25]*2/100, y[x < 25], col=7, cex=1.5)

   lines(c(0.6-0.03, 1.1), c(0, 0))
   axis(1, at=0.6+(pretty(c(75, 100))-75)*2/100, labels=pretty(c(75, 100)),
     ticks=F)
   points(0.6+(x[x > 75]-75)*2/100, y[x > 75], col=7, cex=1.5)

   text.default(c(0.5+0.03, 0.6-0.03), c(0, 0), labels=c('/', '/'))

