Hello S-PLUSERs,

I used to prefer printed manuals until I got
addicted to HTML manuals for other unrelated
software products.  These have the advantage
of being kept up to date by the vendor and are
machine searchable.

When hypertext links are provided, HTML manuals
are a positive pleasure to use.  This is especially
true when they link to functional examples in
applets or active server pages - either offline
or online.

Manuals that are in HTML format can be
readily downloaded or FTP'd after receiving
an update notice.  Further, programs such
as WORD97 facilitate editing and the insertion
of notes.  If you're into programming S-PLUS from
the command window, then copying and pasting
examples make experimenting alot easier.

I dislike Portable Document Files (*.PDF) because
PDF viewers don't offer the scrolling and magnification
ease of INTERNET EXPLORER 5 browsers.  Further,
I don't think that *.PDF files are user-editable.

For these reasons, I vote for HTML manuals that we
can download.

