From rolf at maths.uwa.edu.au  Thu May 13 03:27:22 1999
From: rolf at maths.uwa.edu.au (Rolf Turner)
Date: Thu, 13 May 1999 11:27:22 +0800
Subject: [S] Noncentral t
In-Reply-To: <4130D5B1F917D21189F1080009FCC209D99F12@lsmail.crn.nestrd.ch>
Message-ID: <199905130327.LAA22372@mirbelia.maths.uwa.edu.au>

Marcel Baumgartner wrote:

> thanks to Nciole DePriest Dumars (ntd at stat.ohio-state.edu), who writes:

>> Use the fact that [t(v,delta)]^2 = F(1,v,delta)
>> where v is the degrees of freedom and delta is the
>> noncentrality parameter.

Am I being obtuse?  I don't think this helps much at all.

Suppose that X ~ t(v,delta); then indeed X^2 ~ F(1,v,delta).

Now suppose you want P(X <= x), for x >= 0, say, --- how do you get
it from the available information about X^2?  For the central t
we could get this from the central F as

        0.5*(1 + P(X^2 <= x^2))

using the fact that the central t is symmetric about 0.  But of
course the non-central t ***isn't*** symmetric about anything.

                                cheers,

                                        Rolf Turner
                                        rolf at math.unb.ca
