From agalecki at umich.edu  Thu May 13 12:38:21 1999
From: agalecki at umich.edu (Andrzej Tadeusz Galecki)
Date: Thu, 13 May 1999 08:38:21 -0400
Subject: [S] Summary: Lines in trellis plots (Splus3.4 vs Splus5 on Sun)
In-Reply-To: <Pine.GSO.3.96.990512102908.424W-100000@jaques>
References: <Pine.GSO.3.96.990512102908.424W-100000@jaques>
Message-ID: <Pine.SOL.4.05.9905130813380.22197-100000@tempest.rs.itd.umich.edu>

My thanks go to Bill Dunlap <bill at statsci.com> and Don MacQueen
<macq at llnl.gov>.

Questions:

1. Why the code below works properly in Splus 3.4 and gives error message
in Splus5 on Sun.

> > panel.special <- function(x,y,subscripts){
> > panel.xyplot(x,y)
> > 
> >   for ( i in unique(id)) {
> > cndt <-id[subscripts]==i & base[subscripts] ==1
> > x1 <- x[cndt]
> > y1 <- y[cndt]
> > lines(x1,y1, type="l", lty=2)
> > }}
> > 
> > xyplot(dep~rep.base | agegrp.factor*loc.factor,
> > panel=panel.special,
> >    layout=c(2,3),aspect=1, ylab="IGF",
> >    subscripts=T)

Thorough response from Bill Dunlap:

> It is a bit hard to say without the data, but here is one possibility.
> In Splus 3.4 if one argument to a binary operators (e.g, "==", "&", and "*") 
> had length(0) the result was a vector of NA's the length of the other
> argument.  Thus 13 == integer(0) was NA.  In 5.0 if one argument is 0
> long the result is 0 long so 13 == integer(0) is logical(0).
> 
> Hence if subscripts is zero long in
>   cndt <-id[subscripts]==i & base[subscripts] ==1
> then id[subscripts] will be 0 long and cndt will be zero 0 in Splus5.
> In Splus5 if subscripts is zero long then cndt will be a single NA.
> In both versions
>       lines(x=NA,y=NA)
> works but
>       lines(x=numeric(0), y=numeric(0))
> fails with that error about 0 long arguments in old-S functions.
> 
> Your function fails before this point, but a similar problem may be
> cropping up in panel.xyplot().

2. How to go around this problem?

> > x1 <- x[cndt]
> > y1 <- y[cndt]
    if (length(x1)>0)
> > lines(x1,y1, type="l", lty=2)

Thank you

Andrzej

