>>From Rod Tjoelker
If you want to only pull out specific columns of a dataframe 
or a list, you can index it like a vector.  Here's a function
that will look through a dataframe or list and return the
name of the column (in the list name) and the factor levels
and counts.

sumfun <- function(x)
{
        id <- sapply(x, is.factor)
        out <- lapply(x[id], summary)
        return(out)
}

_____________________________________________________________________
>>From Doug Bates
A simple idiom for determining the class of variables in a data frame is
 sapply(myData, data.class)

If you want to find those that are factors (or ordered factors) use
 findFactors <- function( dat ) {
   v <- sapply(dat, inherits, name = "factor")
   names(v[v])
 }

For example
> sapply(Orthodont, data.class)
 distance       age   Subject       Sex 
"numeric" "numeric" "ordered"  "factor" 
> findFactors( Orthodont )
[1] "Subject" "Sex"
_____________________________________________________________________
And from Chuck Taylor at STATSCI
"describe" <- function(df)
  {

    attach(df)
    on.exit(detach("df"))
    if (version$major < 5 ) my.class <- data.class else my.class <- class
    ColumnClass <- sapply(df, my.class)
    print(ColumnClass)
    for (i in names(ColumnClass)) {
      if (ColumnClass[i]=="factor") {
        cat("\n Factor Variable:  ", i, "\n")
        print(levels(get(i)))
#       or maybe:
#        print(summary(get(i)))
      }
    }
    invisible()
  }

