You can control the height of the strip text (and the height of the strips)
with something like:

par.strip.text=list(cex=0.8)

As for (2), this comes up frequently.  Several of us have our own strip
functions which have an addition style option.  It is most annoying that
StatSci do not incorporate such an additional option.  In R, this would
be fixed in no time.  Here an additional style option

 my.strip
function(which.given, which.panel, var.name, factor.levels,
shingle.intervals, 
        par.strip.text = trellis.par.get("add.text"), strip.names = c(F, T), 
style = 
        7, clip = NULL)
{
        ..................................
                n <- length(factor.levels)
                switch(style,
                        {
........................
                        ,
                        {
# 7 = color-free background
                                color <- 0
                                polygon(c(0, 1, 1, 0), c(0, 0, 1, 1), border = 
F, col
                                   = color)
                                do.call("text", c(list(x = 0.5, y = 0.5, labels 
= 
                                  strip.names), par.strip.text))
                        }
                        )
...................
}

One makes the call with   strip=my.strip

The only change from the function as supplied is to put in the switch
on style = 7, which I have made the default.

For tick labels and axis labels, here is what you can do:

xyplot(Ref~Age, data=ht5, scale=list(x=list(cex=1.8)),xlab=list("XXX",cex=2))

The cex in the scale argument increases the size of the tick labels.
The cex in the xlab argument increases the size of the axis label "XXX".

