From fharrell at virginia.edu  Fri May 14 14:32:35 1999
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Fri, 14 May 1999 10:32:35 -0400
Subject: =?utf-8?q?=5BS=5D_Re=3A_R=C3=A9f=2E_=3A_Re=3A_=5BS=5D_Listing_the_factor_variables_and_labels_of_variables_in_a_large_d_ataframe?=
Message-ID: <005601be9e16$9c1f03e0$5a6c8f80@fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu>

I use a "label" attribute for variables throughout the Hmisc and Design 
libraries.
I attach labels to variables using either the sas.get function (which 
automatically
pulls labels in from SAS), the cleanup.import function (which has an option to
read a SAS PROC CONTENTS CNTLOUT= dataset to get labels), or more commonly
by using label(x) <- 'label', where label() is in Hmisc.  Another way to assign 
labels in
Hmisc is to type Label(dataframe name) which produces output like the following:

> Label(sex.age.response)
label(sex) <- ''
label(age) <- ''
label(response) <- ''

The user types labels inside the pairs of single quotes and runs the commands.
If labels already existed, these will be written and the user can modify them.

Labels (as well as units and imputed attributes) are replayed by the Hmisc 
describe()
function, and I use labels as default axis labels in most of my high-level 
plotting routines.
[If a label is not defined, I use the variable name.]  I have found that the 
trouble of
setting up nice labels permanently in the data frame pays off throughout all 
later
analyses.

I hope that others who don't use Hmisc or Design will also find that the use of
attr(x,'label') is quite helpful, as well as attr(x,'units').

