Received a clear and helpful resonse, which I will post in case anyone
else had the same question.  Thanks

"Z. Todd Taylor" wrote:
> 
> > I am trying to examine similarity of objects
> > with the cluster analysis functions in Splus. I type
> >
> > >plclust(hclust(dist(data.matrix(BZD.DF))))
> >
> > where "BZD.DF" is a data.frame with 18 rows with one object per row and
> > several measurements (1 to 30) for each object distributed across the
> > columns.  Although the clustering pattern seems to match groupings one
> > would intuitively expect for these objects, I am having trouble
> > understanding the meaning of the vertical axis on the resulting plots.
> > Specifically, I don't understand why the objects are not located at
> > distance 0 on the tree plot, with the vertical lines representing
> > distances between objects and clusters.  I thought each object (or
> > minimal cluster) should be at 0 because it's distance from itself is 0.
> > Also, why are some objects at negative height, even though all
> > measurements are positive?
> >
> > Can someone explain this?
> > I would also appreciate any good "starter" refs related to cluster
> > analysis.
> 
> The vertical axis does not relate to the "objects" or
> "clusters"; it relates to the distances between pairs of such.
> A single object can have no "distance" associated with it
> (distance to what?).
> 
> Look at where two clusters merge against the Y-axis.  The only
> zeroes should be where two identical rows merge.  There are no
> negative distances---only the cluster labels fall below zero,
> not the merge points.  See illustration below.
> 
> Hope that helps.
> 
> --Todd
> 
>                     .......................
>                     .                     .
>                     .                     .
>      6..            .                  ........
>        .            .                  .      .
>        .            .                  .      .
>        .            .                  .      .
>        .    .................          .      .
>        .    .          ..........      .   ..........
>      4..    .          .      .....   ...  . .      .
>        .    .        ......   .   .   . .  . .      .
