> relationships between the response variable and the group of explanatory
> variables.  In RPART, I've been using ANOVA as the method and am not sure if

It is the response that determines the method needed: you want
a sum-of-squares criterion, so "anova" (l/case) is appropriate.

> this method is appropriate give the mix of explanatory variables.  I also
> find that I'm unable to specify the na.action = na.rpart option in the
> formual statement without drawing an error statement:
>  Error in .C("s_to_rp",: subroutine s_to_rp: 1939 NaN value(s) in argument
> 10 Dumped.

It is likely that your NAs are really NaNs. Did you import these data?
(Import Data tends to give NaNs.) Try, for a data frame df,

lapply(df, is.nan)

to see.  If so, you need to convert them to NAs (write.table followed by
read.table may be the easiest way.)

